  data:  # aab3
    kind: invariant  # aab3
    title: "post-run cache eviction has no inter-process guard: two concurrent runs over the cap race on the .evicting-/.zip.partial renames and the loser exits 1 with an internal FileNotFoundError after writing every artifact"  # aab3
    status: violated  # aab3
    priority: 2  # aab3
    parent:  # aab3
    tags:  # aab3
    - cache  # aab3
    - cli  # aab3
    - concurrency  # aab3
    isbefore: []  # aab3
    duplicate_of: []  # aab3
    not_duplicate_of: []  # aab3
    pr_ref:  # aab3
    description: "FOUND 2026-10-17 on dev 38f6c90533 while re-verifying tracker items with five fixture-scale `hypergumbo survey` runs in parallel (two independent sub-agents hit it; logs under the session scratchpad, verify-B2/*/survey.log).\n\nWHAT. A run that has already written every artifact exits 1 with `hypergumbo: internal error: FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] ... results/<state> -> results/.evicting-<state>` (three runs) or `... soft-deleted-surveys/<repo>__<state>.zip.partial -> ....zip` (one run). A sequential re-run exits 0 with identical output. The cache was at 5.0 GB, i.e. at the WI-sidin cap, so every run was evicting.\n\nMECHANISM. cli.py `_maybe_evict_cache` runs at the end of every analysis run (call at cli.py:1439) with no inter-process lock. Two concurrent runs over the cap collect the same candidate list; the first renames an entry to its `.evicting-` scratch name and removes it; the second's `cache_rename(entry_path, scratch)` in `_archive_entry` (cli.py:~4379) raises FileNotFoundError, and so can its `cache_rename(partial, final)` when both processes staged the same `.zip.partial`. Nothing in `_archive_entry` / `_maybe_evict_cache` catches it, so it reaches the top-level handler and the user sees \"This is a bug\" and a non-zero exit for a run whose outputs are complete.\n\nVIOLATED INVARIANT. Cache maintenance after a run is best-effort housekeeping: it must never turn a successful run into a failed one, and concurrent runs over the cap must not fail each other. (WI-sidin's docstring argues crash-safety for ONE interrupted process; it does not consider two live ones.)\n\nREPRO. With ~/.cache/hypergumbo at or over the cap, run `hypergumbo survey <fixture-a> --out a.json & hypergumbo survey <fixture-b> --out b.json & wait` on two tiny fixtures; one of the two exits 1 with the FileNotFoundError above. Four parallel runs reproduced it in four of four.\n\nFIX SHAPE. (1) Take a non-blocking exclusive lock (flock on `<cache>/.evict.lock`) around the eviction pass and skip the pass when another process holds it, the way smart-test guards itself (WI-sinap). (2) Independently, treat FileNotFoundError on the entry rename and on the partial->final rename as \"already evicted by someone else\" and continue, so the pass is tolerant even without the lock. (3) Wrap the whole post-run housekeeping call so that any exception there is reported as a warning, not as an internal error, once the artifacts are written. Add a two-process test for (2) and a unit test that a raised FileNotFoundError in `_archive_entry` does not change the run's exit code."  # aab3
    fields:  # aab3
      statement: "Post-run cache housekeeping is best-effort: a run whose artifacts are written never exits non-zero because of eviction, and concurrent runs over the cap never fail each other."  # aab3
      root_cause: "cli.py _maybe_evict_cache runs at the end of every run (cli.py:1439) with no inter-process lock; two processes collect the same candidates, and the second's cache_rename to the .evicting- scratch name (or of the shared .zip.partial) raises FileNotFoundError, which _archive_entry does not catch, so it surfaces as 'internal error' exit 1."  # aab3
